    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The valve body seems like a possibility, but I checked the fluid and it looks pretty clean, nothing obvious like metal shavings or burnt smell. The spikes are almost rhythmic, like they happen every few seconds regardless of RPM. Could it be an electrical issue with the solenoids, or is there something else that would cause that kind of timing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Rhythmic hydraulic pressure spikes in the automatic transmission of a Ford F-150, characterized by regularity and unaffected by RPM variations, could indeed be symptomatic of an electrical issue relating to the solenoids. The timing suggests a possible consistent cause, such as a failing solenoid, a sticking or worn solenoid, or a wiring issue that sends incorrect signals to the solenoid. Maintenance of electrical components, including solenoids and electrical connections, should not be overlooked. An inspection focusing on electrical continuity tests, resistance checks, and solenoid operation with a Hall-effect sensor can conclusively determine if an electrical component is causing the irregular pressure fluctuations. Additionally, check for any signs of voltage drops or false signals from the transmission control module (TCM).",
