    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'll check the solenoids and the TCM next. It's just weird though, everything looks physically fine, yet the pressure keeps jumping around like something's messing with the timing. Is there any way a mechanical part could cause something that looks almost like a phantom signal or just unexplained glitching?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Although mechanical parts don’t typically exhibit what could be perceived as phantom or glitchy activity, there are still several mechanical causes that can result in atypical symptoms appearing as erratic or unexpected behaviour in the hydraulic circuit. Mechanical interference such as loosened bolts or bushings in the valve body, chewed solenoid valves, or a cracked throttle body that intermittently allows air could manifest in pressure spikes or the unusual patterns you are observing. Thrust-to-yaw flange problems leading to intermittent hydraulic leaks, which might occur after very short intervals, can also alter pressure readings. Further mechanical inspection is advisable, looking especially for any cracked components, dislodged parts, or anything that might hinder the smooth operation of movable parts in the transmission. Lubrication for moving parts, the tightening of loose components, or replacing worn parts such as the transmission's solenoids may resolve the issue if it is this less common mechanical cause.",
